# Kotlin

## Format and tooling

- Match the project's Kotlin language version and Gradle / Android settings.
  Do not raise the toolchain for one file.
- Format with the repo's Kotlin formatter (`ktlint`, Spotless, or IDE
  defaults). Do not hand-align beyond what the formatter keeps.
- Fix new compiler and lint findings on code you change. Do not
  `@Suppress` a lint to land the change unless the module already
  suppresses that lint for the same reason.
- Prefer the standard library and the project's existing AndroidX /
  Jetpack libraries. Do not add a dependency for a helper already on the
  classpath.

## Null safety and types

- Prefer non-null types. Use `?` only when absence is a real domain state.
- Prefer `?.` / `?:` / early `return` over `!!` on a path a caller can
  handle. `!!` is allowed only when the invariant is locally obvious.
- Prefer `data class`, `sealed class` / `sealed interface`, and value
  classes for domain models. Do not encode variants as a pile of nullable
  fields.
- Prefer `val` unless reassignment is required. Prefer immutable
  collections at public boundaries.
- Keep Java interop nullability honest (`@Nullable` / `@NonNull` on the
  Java side, or platform-type handling at the boundary).

## Coroutines and UI

- Prefer structured concurrency (`coroutineScope`, `supervisorScope`,
  viewModelScope / lifecycleScope the project already uses) over global
  `GlobalScope`.
- Cancel work when the owner is cleared. Do not launch a fire-and-forget
  coroutine that outlives its screen without an explicit owner.
- Switch dispatchers at the boundary (`withContext`) — do not hardcode
  `Dispatchers.IO` deep inside pure domain code when the project injects
  dispatchers.
- Prefer the project's existing UI stack (Jetpack Compose or Views). Do
  not introduce a second UI toolkit for one screen.
- Do not update UI state off the main thread. Use the project's main
  dispatcher / Compose snapshot rules.

## Modules and packaging

- Keep packages named for what they provide, not `util` or `common`.
- Prefer `internal` for module-local API. Expose only what another module
  needs.
- One primary type per file when the file would otherwise mix networking,
  persistence, and presentation.
- Match the project's Gradle module boundaries. Do not create a new
  module for a single helper.

## Tests

- Prefer the project's test stack (JUnit, Kotest, instrumentation). Ship a
  unit test for a new exported behavior in the same change
  (`code-unit-testing`).
- Cover the failure path and one success path. Prefer fakes or the
  project's existing test doubles over a heavyweight mock framework for a
  single call.
- Do not `Thread.sleep` to wait for a coroutine. Use `runTest`, Turbine,
  or the project's existing async test helper.
